// `*x` over a parameter WRITTEN `&mut i64` is refused BY NAME: there is nothing
// to dereference, because the parameter is already read as its referent.
//
// Code generation reads a `&T` parameter as `(*x)` wherever the body names it,
// and the type checker typed `*e` as `e` ("assume dereference returns the same
// type / TODO"). So `*x` emitted `(*((*x)))` — two indirections through one
// pointer. MEASURED on d5166e8 before this refusal: "gcc compilation failed",
// for this program and for `*m` over a plain `i64` alike. The receiver's own
// form of the same mistake, `*self`, is PD0050. Naming the parameter (`x`)
// reads the value.
fn bump(x: &mut i64) {
    *x = *x + 1;
}

fn main() {
    let mut m: i64 = 5;
    bump(&mut m);
    print_int(m);
}
